    "evidence": [
      {
        "evidence_id": "E001",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "Appeal from an order of the Supreme Court (Connolly, J), entered May 3, 2011 in Albany County, which granted defendants’ motion for summary judgment dismissing the complaint.",
        "statement_status": "procedural_history"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E002",
        "passage_id": "P004",
        "quote": "Ordered that the order is affirmed, with costs.",
        "statement_status": "current_disposition"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E003",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "Plaintiff Andrea Levin (hereinafter plaintiff) was shopping in defendants’ store in the Town of Colonie, Albany County when *1181 she stepped on a strawberry near the bakery section, causing her to slip and fall.",
        "statement_status": "allegation"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E004",
        "passage_id": "P002",
        "quote": "To that end, defendants submitted the deposition testimony of Gregory Fernau, the store manager, that either he and/or his co-managers generally conducted inspections by walking through the store approximately every hour. Fernau testified that he observed the floor area where plaintiff fell within an hour before her fall and that it was clear and dry. Fernau and Ann Marie Pontari, a bakery department employee, both testified that they had not received any complaints about strawberries on the floor at any time.",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E005",
        "passage_id": "P003",
        "quote": "Plaintiffs failed to offer any evidence that defendants created the condition (see Cerkowski v Price Chopper Operating Co., Inc., 68 AD3d at 1384 ). There was no evidence that any of defendants’ employees dropped the strawberry, and plaintiffs’ theory that it was dropped by a bakery employee while retrieving strawberries from the produce department is sheer speculation. Nor did plaintiffs proffer any evidence that defendants had actual or constructive knowledge of the strawberry on the floor, as they have not demonstrated that the condition was visible, apparent and existed for a sufficient period of time for defendants to have discovered and remedied the situation (see Hagin v Sears, Roebuck & Co., 61 AD3d 1264, 1266 [2009]). While plaintiff and Fernau both acknowledged that, after plaintiff fell, they observed another smeared patch of strawberry on the floor *1182 near where she fell, plaintiffs provided no evidence — other than Fernau’s testimony that the area was clear and dry within an hour of the incident — of the length of time the strawberries were present on the floor.",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E006",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "We affirm.",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
